---
import Gallery from '../../layouts/Gallery.astro';
import Related from '../../components/Related.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---
<Gallery title="Pattern: issued document">
  <p class="demo-note">
    <strong>Who uses it</strong>: nobody, on screen — this is the artefact
    the ERP <em>emits</em>: a purchase order sent to a vendor, an invoice, a
    delivery note travelling with goods. <strong>How often</strong>: every
    time a document is issued, usually unattended.
    <strong>What "done" looks like</strong>: a page a stranger can read
    without your system — sender, recipient, document number, lines,
    totals, terms — that survives pagination and a black-and-white printer.
    <strong>Not a screen, and not a PDF engine</strong> — if a person needs
    to work with the record, that is the
    <a href={base + '/patterns/object-page'}>object page</a>; and rendering
    the actual PDF is your server's job (see the grill verdict below), not
    this framework's.
  </p>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Does this belong in a CSS framework at all? — the grill verdict</h2>
    <p>The Accept for this pattern asked the question outright, and the
    honest answer is <strong>half yes</strong>, so it is written down rather
    than assumed:</p>
    <ul class="bo-stack bo-stack--tight docs-list">
      <li><strong>Not ours</strong>: producing the PDF. Headless Chrome,
      Gotenberg, wkhtmltopdf, a print server — that is infrastructure, it
      belongs to your stack, and a CSS framework that shipped one would be
      the <a href={base + '/concepts/scale'}>virtualiser mistake</a> again.</li>
      <li><strong>Ours</strong>: what every one of those tools consumes —
      <em>HTML and a print stylesheet</em>. The document's shape, the page
      contract (<code>@page</code> margins, repeating table headers and
      totals, break control) and the black-and-white legibility rules are
      exactly this framework's job, and they already ship in
      <a href={base + '/base/print'}>the print layer</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>So this page documents the contract</strong>, not a
      generator: give your renderer this markup and these classes, and the
      output is a document. Zero new CSS was needed to build it — which is
      itself the evidence the contract was already complete.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>The document</h2>
    <article class="bo-print-report" style="max-inline-size: 46rem; border: 1px solid var(--bo-color-border-default); padding: var(--bo-space-6); border-radius: var(--bo-radius-md)">
      <header class="bo-u-avoid-break" style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; gap: var(--bo-space-6)">
        <div>
          <p style="margin: 0; font-weight: var(--bo-font-weight-semibold)">Busy Office Supplies Ltd</p>
          <p class="bo-u-text-muted" style="margin: 0">14 Harbour Road · Rotterdam · NL<br />VAT NL0012 3456 B01</p>
        </div>
        <div style="text-align: end">
          <h3 style="margin: 0">Purchase order</h3>
          <p class="bo-u-text-muted" style="margin: 0">PO-88213<br />Issued 22 Aug 2026</p>
        </div>
      </header>

      <div class="bo-form-row bo-u-avoid-break" style="margin-block: var(--bo-space-5)">
        <div>
          <p class="bo-u-text-muted" style="margin: 0">Vendor</p>
          <p style="margin: 0">Acme Supply Co.<br />221 Industrial Way<br />Manchester · UK</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="bo-u-text-muted" style="margin: 0">Deliver to</p>
          <p style="margin: 0">Dock 4, 123 Warehouse Rd<br />Rotterdam · NL</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <dl class="bo-kv bo-u-avoid-break">
        <div><dt>Payment terms</dt><dd>Net 30</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Incoterms</dt><dd>DAP Rotterdam</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Requested delivery</dt><dd>15 Sep 2026</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Buyer</dt><dd>J. Kim</dd></div>
      </dl>

      {/* The container is a SCREEN affordance: on a phone the document's
          lines scroll sideways like a PDF viewer instead of pushing the
          whole page wide. Paged media doesn't scroll, so on paper it is
          inert — the thead/tfoot repetition claims below are about the
          table element and are unaffected. */}
      <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0" style="margin-block-start: var(--bo-space-5)">
      <table class="bo-data-table" data-density="compact">
        <caption class="bo-visually-hidden">Order lines for PO-88213</caption>
        <thead><tr>
          <th scope="col">Material</th>
          <th scope="col">Description</th>
          <th scope="col" class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">Qty</th>
          <th scope="col" class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">Unit price</th>
          <th scope="col" class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">Net value</th>
        </tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="bo-data-table__col--code">MAT-4471</td><td>Hydraulic pump, 24V</td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">12</td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">770.00</td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">9,240.00</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="bo-data-table__col--code">MAT-1180</td><td>Seal kit, 60 mm</td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">40</td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">37.01</td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">1,480.50</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="bo-data-table__col--code">MAT-9002</td><td>Filter cartridge</td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">150</td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">54.80</td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">8,220.00</td></tr>
        </tbody>
        <tfoot>
          <tr><td colspan="4">Net total</td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">18,940.50</td></tr>
          <tr><td colspan="4">VAT 21%</td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">3,977.51</td></tr>
          <tr><td colspan="4"><strong>Total EUR</strong></td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric"><strong>22,918.01</strong></td></tr>
        </tfoot>
      </table>
      </div>

      <footer class="bo-u-avoid-break" style="margin-block-start: var(--bo-space-5)">
        <p class="bo-u-text-muted" style="margin: 0">Terms: goods remain the
        property of the seller until paid in full. Quote PO-88213 on all
        correspondence and invoices.</p>
        <p class="bo-u-print-only">Page totals continue overleaf · PO-88213</p>
      </footer>
    </article>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">On paper this is one document, not a
    screenshot of a screen: the table header repeats on every page and so
    do the totals (<code>&lt;tfoot&gt;</code> is
    <code>table-footer-group</code> by browser default — measured, not
    assumed, which is why the framework adds no rule for it), the address
    blocks never split (<code>.bo-u-avoid-break</code>), and the print-only
    line appears only on paper.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Logo &amp; barcode — any mark, any symbology</h2>
    <p>Both are supported the same way, and the split is the same as the
    PDF one: <strong>the framework does not generate either</strong> —
    barcode bitmaps come from a library (bwip-js, ZXing, a GS1 service) or
    a barcode font, and a logo is your asset. What decides whether the
    printed result actually <em>works</em> is placement, and that is
    entirely CSS:</p>
    <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0">
      <table class="bo-data-table" data-density="compact">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Rule</th><th scope="col">Why it decides success</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>.bo-u-print-exact</code> on the mark</td><td><strong>The one that breaks scanning.</strong> Browsers lighten fills and drop backgrounds when printing; a barcode whose bars come back grey does not scan, and a logo loses its mark. This utility says "these colours are content".</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Quiet zone — blank margin around the symbol</td><td>Every symbology requires one (roughly 10× the narrow-bar width for Code 128, 4 modules for QR). A barcode flush against a table border is unreadable to the scanner even though it looks fine.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Physical units (<code>mm</code>), not <code>px</code></td><td>A barcode has a real-world minimum size. <code>px</code> is a screen unit and its printed size depends on the renderer's DPI assumption; <code>mm</code> means the same thing on every printer.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>SVG for the symbol where possible</td><td>Vector bars stay crisp at any DPI. For a raster barcode add <code>image-rendering: pixelated</code> so the renderer does not smooth the bar edges into each other.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>.bo-u-avoid-break</code> on the block</td><td>A symbol split across a page boundary is two unreadable halves.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Human-readable text under the symbol</td><td>Both the two-channel rule and the actual GS1 standard: the number must be printed as text as well, so a human (or a failed scan) can still read it.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="bo-cluster bo-u-avoid-break" style="gap: var(--bo-space-6); align-items: flex-start; margin-block-start: var(--bo-space-4)">
      <figure class="bo-u-print-exact bo-u-avoid-break" style="margin: 0; text-align: center">
        {/* Stand-in for YOUR barcode image: bars drawn with a gradient so the
            demo needs no asset. Real documents put an <img> or inline <svg>
            here — the placement rules are what this page is documenting. */}
        <div role="img" aria-label="Barcode for document PO-88213" style="inline-size: 48mm; block-size: 14mm; background: repeating-linear-gradient(90deg, #000 0 1.2px, #000 1.2px 2px, transparent 2px 3.4px, #000 3.4px 4.2px, transparent 4.2px 6px); padding: 0; border: 3mm solid #fff"></div>
        <figcaption style="font-family: var(--bo-font-mono); font-size: var(--bo-font-size-xs)">PO-88213</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="bo-u-print-exact" style="margin: 0">
        <div role="img" aria-label="Busy Office Supplies logo" style="inline-size: 26mm; block-size: 26mm; border-radius: var(--bo-radius-md); background: linear-gradient(135deg, var(--bo-color-accent-solid), var(--bo-color-accent-strong)); display: grid; place-items: center; color: #fff; font-weight: var(--bo-font-weight-semibold)">BO</div>
        <figcaption class="bo-u-text-muted" style="font-size: var(--bo-font-size-xs); text-align: center">Logo · 26mm</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <pre><code>{`<!-- Barcode: your renderer supplies the image; these classes and
     units are what make the printed result scan. -->
<figure class="bo-u-print-exact bo-u-avoid-break">
  <img src="/barcode/PO-88213.svg" alt="Barcode for document PO-88213"
       style="inline-size: 48mm; block-size: 14mm; padding: 3mm" />
  <figcaption>PO-88213</figcaption>   <!-- human-readable, always -->
</figure>

<!-- Logo: same utility, physical size, real alt text. -->
<img class="bo-u-print-exact" src="/brand/logo.svg"
     alt="Busy Office Supplies Ltd" style="inline-size: 26mm" />`}</code></pre>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">This closes a loop the framework already had
    open on the other side: <code>initScanInput()</code> on the
    <a href={base + '/patterns/goods-receipt'}>RF goods-receipt screen</a>
    <em>reads</em> these barcodes off a handheld. The document that gets
    scanned in the warehouse is the one this pattern emits — emit and read
    are now both documented.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Page numbers &amp; running headers — what browsers do, and what needs a renderer</h2>
    <p>Compared against the dedicated paged-media engines
    (<a href="https://www.princexml.com/doc/paged/">Prince</a>,
    <a href="https://doc.courtbouillon.org/weasyprint/stable/">WeasyPrint</a>),
    which is what the CSS Paged Media and GCPM specs were written for.
    Two of their headline features matter for an ERP document, and they do
    <em>not</em> land the same way — measured here by printing a
    three-page table to PDF and reading the text back out, not by
    consulting a support table:</p>
    <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0">
      <table class="bo-data-table" data-density="compact">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Feature</th><th scope="col">Browser print / headless Chrome</th><th scope="col">Prince · WeasyPrint</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><code>@page</code> margin box with <code>counter(page)</code> / <code>counter(pages)</code></td>
            <td><span class="bo-badge bo-badge--success">Works</span> — rendered "1 of 3", "2 of 3", "3 of 3" on the right pages</td>
            <td>Works</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>string-set</code> + <code>content: string(…)</code> running headers</td>
            <td><span class="bo-badge bo-badge--danger">No</span> — emitted nothing at all in the same test</td>
            <td>Works (GCPM)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Repeating <code>&lt;thead&gt;</code> / <code>&lt;tfoot&gt;</code></td>
            <td><span class="bo-badge bo-badge--success">Works</span> — header and totals repeated on all 3 pages</td>
            <td>Works</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>So <strong>page numbering is available to you today</strong>, in a
    plain browser, with no renderer and no JavaScript. It is not shipped as
    a framework default because <code>@page</code> is a
    <em>document-global</em> rule — it cannot be scoped to one route or one
    element, so shipping it would stamp page numbers on every print job in
    every consuming app, including screens where that is wrong. It belongs
    in the stylesheet of your document route:</p>
    <pre><code>{`/* your-document-route.css — paste as-is; verified in Chrome */
@page {
  margin: 1.6cm;                       /* the framework already sets this */
  @bottom-center {
    content: "Page " counter(page) " of " counter(pages);
    font-size: 9pt;
  }
  @bottom-right { content: "PO-88213"; }   /* the doc number on every page */
}

/* Server-renderer ONLY (Prince, WeasyPrint) — a running header that
   repeats the document title. Browsers ignore string-set entirely, so
   never rely on it for a document a user might print from the app. */
h1 { string-set: doctitle content(); }
@page { @top-center { content: string(doctitle); } }`}</code></pre>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">The document above uses a plain
    <code>.bo-u-print-only</code> line carrying its number instead, because
    that works in <em>both</em> paths — the browser print dialog and the
    server renderer — and a loose page from a printed PO still identifies
    itself. Add the margin-box recipe on top when the document is rendered
    by a route you control.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Markup</h2>
    <pre><code>{`<!-- The whole document is one .bo-print-report. No new classes:
     letterhead + parties + kv meta + lines + tfoot totals + terms. -->
<article class="bo-print-report">
  <header class="bo-u-avoid-break"> …sender / doc number / date… </header>

  <div class="bo-form-row bo-u-avoid-break"> …vendor / deliver-to… </div>

  <dl class="bo-kv bo-u-avoid-break"> …terms, incoterms, buyer… </dl>

  <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0">  <!-- screen: narrow
       viewports scroll the lines like a PDF; print ignores it -->
  <table class="bo-data-table" data-density="compact">
    <thead>…</thead>            <!-- repeats on every printed page -->
    <tbody>…lines…</tbody>
    <tfoot>…net / VAT / total…</tfoot>  <!-- repeats too, by default -->
  </table>
  </div>

  <footer class="bo-u-avoid-break">
    <p>Terms…</p>
    <p class="bo-u-print-only">Page totals continue overleaf · PO-88213</p>
  </footer>
</article>`}</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Anatomy</h2>
    <ol class="bo-stack bo-stack--tight docs-list">
      <li><strong>Letterhead</strong> — who is sending, with the legal
      identifiers (VAT/company number) a document needs to be valid.</li>
      <li><strong>Document identity</strong> — type, number, issue date.
      The number is the thing every later conversation quotes. Not itself
      a monitoring signal — the human-monitoring surface for WHETHER
      documents were emitted lives on <a href={base + '/patterns/report'}>report</a>
      and <a href={base + '/patterns/job-monitor'}>job monitor</a>; this
      region is what makes an artefact self-evidencing once someone does
      open it.</li>
      <li><strong>Parties</strong> — vendor and ship-to as separate blocks,
      each <code>.bo-u-avoid-break</code>: an address split across a page
      boundary is a returned delivery.</li>
      <li><strong>Meta</strong> — <a href={base + '/components/kv'}>key-value
      facts</a> for terms, incoterms, dates, buyer.</li>
      <li><strong>Lines + totals</strong> — a
      <a href={base + '/components/data-table'}>data table</a> with a real
      <code>&lt;tfoot&gt;</code>; both header and totals repeat per page
      because the print layer and the browser default already say so.</li>
      <li><strong>Terms &amp; footer</strong> — the legal tail, plus
      <code>.bo-u-print-only</code> content that exists on paper only.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Data contract</h2>
    <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0">
      <table class="bo-data-table" data-density="compact">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Request</th><th scope="col">Returns / on failure</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="bo-data-table__col--code">GET /po/:id/document</td><td>this HTML, standalone (no app shell, no nav) — a complete page your renderer or a browser can print directly. 404 if the record does not exist; 5xx if rendering itself fails — both as a plain error page, never a broken document that LOOKS complete</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="bo-data-table__col--code">GET /po/:id/document.pdf</td><td>your server runs the SAME HTML through its renderer; the PDF and the printed page cannot disagree because they are one source</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="bo-data-table__col--code">issue / re-issue</td><td>a re-issued document states it ("Copy · re-issued 24 Aug 2026") — an unmarked duplicate of a financial document is a control failure</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="bo-data-table__col--code">draft</td><td>a document that is not yet issued must SAY so on its face (see states) — never render a draft that looks issued</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted"><strong>The AI-manages / human-monitors
    split, stated</strong>: agents generate and dispatch these unattended,
    so the document itself carries every check a human later needs — the
    number, the issue date, the draft/copy marking, the totals. The
    monitoring surface for <em>whether</em> they were emitted is the
    <a href={base + '/patterns/report'}>report</a> and the job monitor; this
    page's job is that each emitted artefact is self-evidencing.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>States</h2>
    <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0">
      <table class="bo-data-table" data-density="compact">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">State</th><th scope="col">What ships</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Draft</td><td>the word DRAFT in the document identity block and in a print-only line — never a colour wash or a watermark image, which a black-and-white printer or a forced-colors reader loses entirely</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Issued</td><td>issue date present; the document is the record of what was sent</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Copy / re-issue</td><td>marked in text with the re-issue date beside the original</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cancelled</td><td>stated in words at the top; the lines stay readable because a cancelled document still has to be auditable</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Multi-page</td><td>header and totals repeat; parties and terms blocks never split; the print-only continuation line carries the document number so a loose page is identifiable</td></tr>
          <tr><td>No lines</td><td>refuse to emit — a document with no lines is a bug upstream, not an empty state to style</td></tr>
          <tr><td>No JS</td><td>fully functional — this page has no JS dependency; it's a rendered, printable document (the <code>initScanInput()</code> mentioned here belongs to the separate goods-receipt screen that later reads its barcode, not to this page)</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Components used</h2>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted"><span class="bo-badge">complexity 2 of 4</span></p>
    <ul class="bo-cluster docs-list-bare">
      <li><a class="bo-badge bo-badge--type" href={base + '/components/data-table'}>Data table</a></li>
      <li><a class="bo-badge bo-badge--type" href={base + '/components/kv'}>Key-value facts</a></li>
      <li><a class="bo-badge bo-badge--type" href={base + '/base/print'}>Print utilities</a></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

